  "forum_transcript": [
    {
      "episode_id": "VR-488593",
      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 check of the Unit 4 electricity readings (1-30 September 2026). Consumption is taken from the actual endpoint readings, as the note says billing uses actual readings only, so the 15 September estimated main-hall figure is not needed: M-0412 = 50,977.0 - 48,213.6 = 2,763.4 kWh; M-0413 = 12,902.9 - 12,040.2 = 862.7 kWh. Total across both meters = 2,763.4 + 862.7 = 3,626.1 kWh, which exceeds the 3,500 kWh threshold by 126.1 kWh. Splitting M-0412 through the estimated mid-month figure gives the same total (995.4 + 1,475.0 = 2,763.4), so the estimate does not change the result either way. Points worth flagging: the main hall alone is 2,763.4 kWh, below the threshold, so the claim only holds because it is stated 'across its two meters'; and the margin is small (126.1 kWh, about 3.5%), so a reading error of that size would matter, but the endpoints are stated to be actual engineer readings with no rollover or meter replacement. Uncertainties: this is the only document in the case, and the readings are as printed. On the record as submitted the claim holds.",
          "content_hash": "0x55e200248a2043ae8454ea3a4732c0698bc0a9e3f9264ad248ec7bfaa67420d3",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321574098,
          "message_id": "7d6aee2b-7658-48af-8d8b-2e1575fef77d",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 check of the Unit 4 electricity readings (Northfield Business Park, September 2026). The claim is that Unit 4 used more than 3,500 kWh in September across its two meters. Consumption must be taken from the actual readings only, per the document: 1 and 30 September are actual, the 15 September main-hall figure is estimated and is not a billing basis. M-0412 main hall: 50,977.0 - 48,213.6 = 2,763.4 kWh. M-0413 annex: 12,902.9 - 12,040.2 = 862.7 kWh. Combined: 2,763.4 + 862.7 = 3,626.1 kWh, which exceeds 3,500 by 126.1 kWh. Two things a reader could get wrong here. First, neither meter alone clears the threshold (2,763.4 and 862.7), so the claim only holds because it says 'across its two meters'; checking the main hall alone would wrongly suggest the claim fails. Second, the 15 September estimate is a red herring: because consumption is the difference between the 1 and 30 September actuals, whether one splits the period at the estimate or not gives the same 2,763.4 kWh for M-0412 (1,288.4 estimated segment plus 1,475.0 actual segment). The document also rules out the usual adjustments: no other meters, and no rollover or meter replacement in the period. Uncertainties: the reading sheet is the only document in the case, and the margin is modest at 126.1 kWh (about 3.6% above the threshold), so a material correction to either actual reading, or an unmetered supply at the unit, would change the answer; nothing in the record suggests either. On the record as submitted the claim holds and I see no basis for a challenge.",
          "content_hash": "0x89e8b2d91bcaec19522bdc5214b1674cfc347c0e3b0cb6bd70cfae04c8dc33da",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321582569,
          "message_id": "2582044c-31ca-40dc-ab05-b2a3f62ba4fe",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-1 independent check of the Unit 4 meter readings (1-30 September 2026). Billing is from actual readings only, so the endpoints govern: M-0412 main hall = 50,977.0 - 48,213.6 = 2,763.4 kWh; M-0413 annex = 12,902.9 - 12,040.2 = 862.7 kWh. Combined across both meters = 2,763.4 + 862.7 = 3,626.1 kWh, which is above the 3,500 kWh threshold by 126.1 kWh (about 3.6%). The 15 September estimated main-hall figure does not affect this: splitting M-0412 at that point gives 1,288.4 + 1,475.0 = 2,763.4 kWh, identical to the endpoint difference, so the estimate is irrelevant either way. Two readings that would give the wrong answer: neither meter alone clears the threshold (2,763.4 and 862.7), so the claim holds only as stated 'across its two meters'; and treating the estimated mid-month figure as a billing basis is unnecessary rather than decisive. The document also excludes the usual adjustments - no other meters, no rollover, no meter replacement in the period. Uncertainties: this is the only document in the case; the endpoints are stated to be actual engineer readings but are taken as printed, and the margin is modest, so a material correction to either actual reading or an unmetered supply at the unit would change the conclusion. On the record as submitted the claim holds.",
          "content_hash": "0x899d30efa0b55a581d8f69a08b2c5fbb9a5286ffefb51f69a3e8af3e23e0e376",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321598422,
          "message_id": "f9d9bb14-1c48-476a-a0a6-9291fff5368f",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Small factual correction to juror-2's note (seq 1), offered in case anyone relies on the intermediate figure. The endpoint totals there are right, but the mid-month split of M-0412 is not: the estimated 15 September reading of 49,502.0 minus the 1 September actual of 48,213.6 is 1,288.4 kWh, not 995.4 kWh, and 50,977.0 minus 49,502.0 is 1,475.0 kWh. So the split is 1,288.4 + 1,475.0 = 2,763.4 kWh, which matches the endpoint difference as it must. (The quoted 995.4 + 1,475.0 would give 2,470.4, which does not reconcile with either endpoint reading, so it looks like a slip rather than a different method.) This does not change juror-2's conclusion or mine: total consumption is 2,763.4 + 862.7 = 3,626.1 kWh, above the 3,500 threshold by 126.1 kWh, and the claim holds on the actual endpoint readings.",
          "content_hash": "0x9a428d6106ceac86a50d477189737bb865ff48a695e26459323816f8c7266c1a",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321599594,
          "message_id": "befd9f16-d9b6-4264-adb4-813ff9fa4920",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 4
        },
        {
          "content": "Checked the Unit 4 readings for 1-30 September 2026. Billing uses actual readings only, so the 15 September estimated main-hall figure is not needed: M-0412 = 50,977.0 - 48,213.6 = 2,763.4 kWh and M-0413 = 12,902.9 - 12,040.2 = 862.7 kWh, a total of 3,626.1 kWh across the two meters, which exceeds 3,500 kWh by 126.1 kWh. Splitting M-0412 at the estimated mid-month figure gives the identical total (1,288.4 + 1,475.0 = 2,763.4), so the estimate does not change the result either way. Two things worth flagging: the main hall alone is 2,763.4 kWh, i.e. below the threshold, so the claim holds only because it is stated 'across its two meters'; and the margin is 126.1 kWh (about 3.5%), so a reading error of that size would matter, though the endpoints are stated to be actual engineer readings with no rollover or meter replacement and no other meters. Uncertainties: this document is the only evidence in the case and the readings are taken as printed. On the record as submitted the claim holds, so no challenge from me.",
          "content_hash": "0x67b3c757a179899d3ede75d9b833b5c9c8113316c712c7df165ba8e4ce7724e4",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321647279,
          "message_id": "1fdad4b7-12b9-4f66-b989-69ffb5980801",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "challenger",
          "sequence": 5
        },
        {
          "content": "Correction accepted - juror-3 is right (seq 4). My seq 1 note said the mid-month split of M-0412 was 995.4 + 1,475.0; that was an error. The correct split through the estimated 15 September reading is 49,502.0 - 48,213.6 = 1,288.4 kWh plus 50,977.0 - 49,502.0 = 1,475.0 kWh, i.e. 2,763.4 kWh, which is what it must be to reconcile with the endpoint difference. My 995.4 figure reconciles with nothing and should be disregarded. This does not change my conclusion: the endpoint readings give M-0412 2,763.4 kWh and M-0413 862.7 kWh, a total of 3,626.1 kWh, which exceeds 3,500 kWh by 126.1 kWh, so the claim holds. The substantive points in my earlier note stand: the estimate is a red herring because it cancels between the actual endpoints, the main hall alone (2,763.4 kWh) is below the threshold so the claim only holds as stated 'across its two meters', and the margin of 126.1 kWh is modest. Thanks to juror-3 for the check.",
          "content_hash": "0xc0f3571a85d17fb2934fc13cee15630e4f2f302357ba65d605fefe6fcb1d88f7",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321705879,
          "message_id": "c1fc82ad-3e5a-4633-8743-9143c05927ea",
          "round_num": 2,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 6
        }
      ],
      "next_after": 6
    }
